<img class="img" src="~/segno.jpg" alt="Portrait of Fiore dei Liberi" />

<p>Before firearms revolutionized the battlefield and self-defense, men needed extensive training to wield their weapons effectively. There existed in all nations basic fencing traditions for sport and war, but for the nobility and clergy who had nothing but time to spend, masters honed and shared their expertise. They catalogued numerous forms and methods of defense and outlined typical scenarios, like aristocrats in sketchy neighborhoods or knights jousting in tournaments, and distributed these fighting manuals for their patrons and friends to study and gain the upper hand in the next duel. Thanks to the dedication and care of generations of enthusiasts, many treatises have survived to modern day for our edification and have already been thoroughly researched and brought to life through sparring. </p>


<img class="img" src="~/plate.jpg" alt="Two fencers fighting from the bind" />

<p>
    Today, Historical European Martial Arts has gained a dedicated cult following as YouTube channels and local clubs spread across the land. From a variety of sources, practitioners learn a bevy of fighting styles, ranging from wrestling to armored combat, and enjoy a totally unique form of exercise and competition. Since sparring can be intimidating and proper protective gear, pricy, clubs prepare loads of loaner gear and beginner classes, so anyone can walk in and immediately begin training. If you are feeling brave, go find your local HEMA chapter and pick up a blade!
</p>